          %h1#examples_of_
            Examples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%B6licher+Spaces"} Frölicher Spaces
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#manifolds_as_frlicher_spaces"} Manifolds as Frölicher Spaces
              %li
                %a{:href => "#the_pinched_plane"} The Pinched Plane
              %li
                %a{:href => "#coequaliser_example"} Coequaliser Example
          %h2#manifolds_as_frlicher_spaces Manifolds as Frölicher Spaces
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            defines a Frölicher space with curves
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(\mathbb{R}, M)
            and functions
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_2.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(M, \mathbb{R})
          %h2#the_pinched_plane The Pinched Plane
          %p Taking quotients in the category of Frölicher spaces is straightforward: the smooth functions are those that pull-back to smooth functions on the original space.
          %p
            As an example, consider the plane
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
            quotiented out by the
            = succeed "-axis." do
              %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          #quotientx{:style => "text-align: center;"}
            %svg{:height => "197", "se:nonce" => "83381", :width => "188", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:se" => "http://svg-edit.googlecode.com", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
              \&lt;!-- Created with SVG-edit - http://svg-edit.googlecode.com/ --&gt;
              %g
                %title Layer 1
                %line#svg_83381_1{:fill => "none", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :x1 => "1", :x2 => "183", :y1 => "1", :y2 => "1"}
                %line#svg_83381_2{:fill => "none", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :x1 => "5", :x2 => "187", :y1 => "195", :y2 => "195"}
                %path#svg_83381_3{:d => "m2,2c2,157 181,35 185,193", :fill => "none", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2"}
                %path#svg_83381_4{:d => "m182,2c5,173 -179,18 -177,194", :fill => "none", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2"}
                %path#svg_83381_5{:d => "m39,2c3,167 107,25 109,192", :fill => "none", :stroke => "#9b9b9b", "stroke-width" => "2"}
                %path#svg_83381_6{:d => "m136,2c5,175 -86,17 -85,193", :fill => "none", :stroke => "#9b9b9b", "stroke-width" => "2"}
                %line#svg_83381_7{:fill => "none", :stroke => "#9b9b9b", "stroke-width" => "2", :x1 => "94", :x2 => "96", :y1 => "2", :y2 => "193.99999"}
          %p
            Let us write this as
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_5.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_6.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            are simple to describe: the set is equivalent to those smooth functions
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_7.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}
            which are constant on the
            = succeed "-axis." do
              %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_8.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            Now let us consider the smooth curves. Let
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_9.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha : \mathbb{R} \to X
            be a smooth curve. We can partition
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_10.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            into two pieces: those points that are mapped to the squashed point in
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_11.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and those points that aren’t. Let us write
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_12.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            for the set of points that are
            %strong not
            mapped to the squashed point. Using bump functions it is easy to show that
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_13.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is open in
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_14.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_15.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2 \to X
            is bijective off the
            = succeed "-axis," do
              %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_16.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_17.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            to
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_18.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            has a unique lift to
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_19.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_20.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_x
            and
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_21.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_y
            for the coordinate functions of this lift. Again using bump functions, it is easy to show that
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_22.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_x
            and
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_23.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_y
            are smooth on
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_24.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_25.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,y) \mapsto y
            descends to a smooth function on
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_26.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_27.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_y
            is actually the restriction to
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_28.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            of a smooth function
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_29.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
            which we shall also denote by
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_30.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_y
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_31.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
