                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \alpha_y^{-1}(0)
          %p
            The interesting part comes when looking at what happens to
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_32.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_x
            at the boundary of
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_33.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_34.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is open, it is a disjoint union of open intervals. The boundaries of each of these intervals forms part of the boundary of
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_35.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and it is simplest to start with these points. For further simplicity, let us assume that
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_36.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,1)
            is one of the components of
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_37.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and we are considering the boundary point
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_38.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_39.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi lim
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mn 0
                          %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim_{t \to 0^+} \alpha_x(t)
          %p
            The general rule is simple to state:
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_40.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_y
            must go to zero faster than
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_41.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_x
            (and any of its derivatives) can go to infinity.
          %h2#coequaliser_example Coequaliser Example
          %p
            Let us give an example that shows that the category of Frölicher spaces is not
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequaliser"} coequaliser
            diagram
            %math#mathml_aa4aa9075830b6d749d1c29a560732ae809d1b7b_42.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ∖
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ⨿
